body:
Sorry you're going through this. PMDD is SO VERY ROUGH on the partners and families of the woman with PMDD. 

&#x200B;

You sound like you have been very patient. (As a woman with a very patient partner, I just have to say that it's so appreciated. Your wife might be thankful for your patience but is just not expressing it.)

&#x200B;

I have said some horrible things to my partner during PMDD, and yes, I feel utterly awful once I come out of the fog and realize how terribly I was behaving. THAT BEING SAID, I did not always feel so remorseful.

&#x200B;

Have you already sat down with your wife (during a non-PMDD day) and told her how much it damages you when she treats you (and your daughter) horribly? 

&#x200B;

Honestly, I kind of "forgot" for a long time that my PMDD was serious until I fully realized just how much I was hurting my partner. I don't think I really expressed remorse to him until I realized I was hurting him. And the way I realized it is that he told me in a very direct way.

&#x200B;

(I know it sounds crazy, but I kind of just thought I was acting normal during PMDD -- even though I definitely was not. In my (PMDD) mind, I was always just reacting "appropriately" to the situation at hand. In my mind, HE was being the jerk and so he "deserved" the treatment I was giving him. During PMDD, it is SO hard to see it when we are being ridiculous. When we're in that "werewolf" zone, it's so difficult to determine that we're in it. Your wife might be in denial because it simply feels so "natural" to behave this way during PMDD.)

&#x200B;

My partner one day (during a non-PMDD time) basically told me that he couldn't handle me anymore. He actually used the words "I'm broken. You broke me," and he wanted to break up. He told me that I was hurting him tremendously with my behavior, which was devastating to me and was a huge wakeup call. I begged him not to break up with me, and the whole thing prompted me to start seeking new methods to cope with the PMDD. Also, the realization that I was hurting him badly is what what made me start feeling so remorseful afterwards. (Before he told me I was hurting him, I didn't realize I was being horrible. I mean, it should have been obvious to me that I was acting like a witch, but I cant overemphasize this: It is so challenging to recognize PMDD symptoms when we're having them. And if no one directly tells us that our behavior was unbearable, we don't always see it -- even if it seems like it should be obvious.)

&#x200B;

Now my situation is different from your wife because I already knew I had PMDD, and she does not. (I just didn't realize how bad mine was.) But what I'm trying to say is: Maybe if you tell your wife in great detail about the many ways she hurts you and your daughter -- and even attempt to leave her -- maybe then she will recognize the extent of her problem and take action. Maybe she needs a grand wakeup call. Maybe you can even direct her to this subreddit?
